Why This Is Asked
Tests practical low-level concurrency skills: non-temporal stores, false sharing avoidance, NUMA awareness, and correct lock-free sequencing.

Key Concepts
- Lock-free ring buffers
- Non-temporal stores
- Cache-line padding to prevent false sharing
- Memory ordering: release/acquire, CAS
- NUMA pinning and microbenchmarking

Code Example
```cpp
// Slot and ring sketch (conceptual)
typedef struct alignas(256) Slot {
  uint64_t seq;
  uint8_t payload[256];
} Slot;

typedef struct {
  Slot *slots;
  _Atomic uint32_t head, tail;
} Ring;

bool ring_push(Ring *r, const void *src){
  uint32_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &r->slots[h & (N-1)];
  uint64_t seq = atomic_load_explicit(&s->seq, memory_order_acquire);
  if (seq != h) return false; // full
  // non-temporal payload write path placeholder
  memcpy(s->payload, src, 256);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, h+1, memory_order_release);
  atomic_store_explicit(&r->head, h+1, memory_order_relaxed);
  return true;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design crash‑consistent data paths in persistent memory, a common production issue when power failures occur during in‑flight operations.

## Key Concepts

- Durability guarantees for PMEM writes
- Memory ordering: release/acquire, fences, cacheline flushes
- Recovery via redo log and slot sequence counters
- ABA avoidance in a concurrent ring
- Testing with fault injection and crash replay

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-code: enqueue/dequeue with PMEM flush/fence
// Slot: { data, seq, valid }
void enqueue(T v){
  size_t t = tail.fetch_add(1);
  Slot *s = &ring[t % CAP];
  s->data = v; // write data
  clwb(&s->data, sizeof(T));
  fence(); // ensure data persists
  s->seq = t; // publish with durable tail update (release)
  clwb(&s->seq, sizeof(size_t));
  fence();
  // publish tail already happens via atomic tail update
}

bool dequeue(T *out){
  size_t h0 = head.load();
  Slot *s = &ring[h0 % CAP];
  if (s->seq != h0) return false;
  *out = s->data;
  s->valid = false;
  clwb(&s->valid, sizeof(bool));
  fence();
  head.fetch_add(1);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle power loss during the publish vs data write steps?
- How would you validate recovery correctness across multiple restart scenarios?

## Why This Is Asked
Low-level timer logic is common in network and game loops; beginner-friendly but tests memory layout, wrap-around, and basic lock-free thinking without heavy concurrency.

## Key Concepts
- Ring buffers and wrap-around
- Pre-allocated object pools
- Scheduling by slot arithmetic and locality
- Minimal locking assumptions in single-threaded loop

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Task { void (*cb)(void*); void* ctx; struct Task* next; } Task;
#define WHEEL_SIZE 256
static Task* wheel[WHEEL_SIZE];
static int cur = 0;
static Task pool[512]; static int pool_free = 512;
void init_wheel(){ for(int i=0;i<WHEEL_SIZE;i++) wheel[i]=NULL; cur=0; pool_free=512; }
static Task* alloc_task(){ return &pool[--pool_free]; }
void schedule(void (*cb)(void*), void* ctx, uint32_t delay_ms){ int slot = (cur + delay_ms) & (WHEEL_SIZE-1); Task* t = alloc_task(); t->cb=cb; t->ctx=ctx; t->next=wheel[slot]; wheel[slot]=t; }
void tick_one_ms(){ Task* t = wheel[cur]; wheel[cur]=NULL; while(t){ Task* next=t->next; t->cb(t->ctx); t = next; } cur=(cur+1)&(WHEEL_SIZE-1); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for multiple producers? 
- How would you test with bursty traffic and verify timing guarantees?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical low-level data structure design under bounded resources, including probing, memory layout, and edge handling (empty slots, full table).

## Key Concepts
- Open addressing with linear probing
- Fixed capacity and sentinel handling
- Separate arrays for keys and values for cache locality
- Return conventions for success/failure

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>

#define N (1<<12)
static uint32_t keys[N];
static uint64_t vals[N];

static inline size_t probe(uint32_t k) { return k % N; }

void ht_init() {
  for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) keys[i] = 0;
}

int ht_insert(uint32_t k, uint64_t v) {
  if (k == 0) return -1; // sentinel reserved
  size_t idx = probe(k);
  for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
    if (keys[idx] == 0) { keys[idx] = k; vals[idx] = v; return 0; }
    if (keys[idx] == k) { vals[idx] = v; return 0; }
    idx = (idx + 1) % N;
  }
  return -1; // full
}

int ht_lookup(uint32_t k, uint64_t* out) {
  if (k == 0) return -1;
  size_t idx = probe(k);
  for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
    if (keys[idx] == 0) return -1;
    if (keys[idx] == k) { *out = vals[idx]; return 0; }
    idx = (idx + 1) % N;
  }
  return -1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you resize or rehash when load factor grows? 
- How to detect and recover from clustering effects in probing? 
- What are alternatives to sentinel-based empties (bitmaps, tombstones)?

